material savings through improved governance and strategic\u2026","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"Contract Award Agency Procurement Platform - Cabinet report","paper_url":"https://walthamforest.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110301/110301.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Commercial property and investment income shortfall from 2025/26 of \u00a30.900m still under review, with bad debt risk emerging","lead":"A \u00a30.900m prior-year commercial income shortfall combined with an incomplete review creates an unquantified downside risk that could materialise mid-year when remediation time is limited.","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Service Narrative","paper_url":"https://walthamforest.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110307/110307.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true},{"headline":"Housing Revenue Account responsive repairs overspend doubled to \u00a32.137m due to Deed of Variation with Morgan Sindall, with further contract risks emerging","lead":"Contract renegotiations can carry hidden costs; the 70% underestimate on the DoV suggests weak commercial controls, and simultaneous retendering of two major contracts exposes the HRA to service disruption and cost spikes.","iso":"2026-09-08","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Service Narrative","paper_url":"https://walthamforest.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110307/110307.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Contract Award Agency Procurement Platform - Cabinet report","paper_url":"https://walthamforest.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110301/110301.pdf","headline":"Agency spend reduced by \u00a37.678m in 2025/26, reaching six-year low of \u00a317.985m","detail":"Waltham Forest achieved a major reduction in agency staffing costs from \u00a325.663m in 2024/25 to \u00a317.985m in 2025/26, with agency headcount falling from 415 in June 2024 to 224 by June 2026. The council attributes this to strengthened governance arrangements and focused workforce controls under the Managed Service Provider partnership model initiated in October 2022.","category":"savings","matters":"A lender needs evidence that a council can execute cost-reduction programs and maintain financial discipline on major expense categories. This demonstrates Waltham Forest has achieved sustained, material savings through improved governance and strategic workforce management.","figures":"\u00a317.985m (2025/26), down from \u00a325.663m (2024/25) and \u00a327.004m (2023/24); agency headcount 224 (June 2026) down from 415 (June 2024); reduction of \u00a37.678m year-on-year","quote":"The council closed 2025/26 with agency spend at \u00a317.985m (down from \u00a325.663m in 2024/25), which was the lowest level recorded in six years.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Service Narrative","paper_url":"https://walthamforest.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110307/110307.pdf","headline":"Commercial property and investment income shortfall from 2025/26 of \u00a30.900m still under review, with bad debt risk emerging","detail":"The Place directorate reports a breakeven position at month 3 but carries a 'potential pressure within Commercial Property & Investment' pending completion of a 2026/27 rental profile review. The 2025/26 outturn showed a shortfall of \u00a30.900m on commercial income. The review is 'nearly complete' but forecasts are not expected to be refined until month 6.","category":"income","matters":"A \u00a30.900m prior-year commercial income shortfall combined with an incomplete review creates an unquantified downside risk that could materialise mid-year when remediation time is limited.","figures":"2025/26 commercial income shortfall \u00a30.900m; review incomplete until month 6","quote":"there is a potential pressure within Commercial Property & Investment - subject to the ongoing full review of the 2026/27 rental profile and potential bad debts. The 2025/26 outturn had a shortfall of \u00a30.900m on commercial income.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Service Narrative","paper_url":"https://walthamforest.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110307/110307.pdf","headline":"Housing Revenue Account responsive repairs overspend doubled to \u00a32.137m due to Deed of Variation with Morgan Sindall, with further contract risks emerging","detail":"The HRA is overspending on repairs by \u00a31.200m overall, driven by responsive repairs overspend of \u00a32.137m. The council renegotiated the Morgan Sindall contract in November 2025 via a Deed of Variation. Originally estimated to cost \u00a31.757m annually (with \u00a30.807m growth already budgeted), the actual impact is now estimated at \u00a33.000m\u2014a \u00a31.243m underestimate. Additional pressures include higher-than-expected Price per Property (PPP) exclusions (~\u00a30.600m). Additionally, the responsive repairs contract expires 30 September 2027 and the mechanical/engineering contract expires 31 March 2027; both are being reproc\u00adured simultaneously, introducing demobilisation and performance risks.","category":"overspend","matters":"Contract renegotiations can carry hidden costs; the 70% underestimate on the DoV suggests weak commercial controls, and simultaneous retendering of two major contracts exposes the HRA to service disruption and cost spikes.","figures":"Responsive repairs overspend \u00a32.137m; Deed of Variation originally \u00a31.757m, now \u00a33.000m (underestimate \u00a31.243m); PPP exclusions pressure ~\u00a30.600m","quote":"Based on experience during the first six months the DoV has been in operation, the annual impact is now estimated to be approximately \u00a33.000m.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Service Narrative","paper_url":"https://walthamforest.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110307/110307.pdf","headline":"Section 17 (Children in Need) spending overspend masked by \u00a30.750m draw from Crisis Resilience Fund; underlying overspend \u00a30.958m","detail":"Children's Services Section 17 is forecast to overspend by \u00a30.208m, but this figure depends on drawing \u00a30.750m from the Crisis and Resilience Fund. Without that draw, the overspend would be \u00a30.958m. This represents spending being funded from a time-limited grant reserve rather than base budget.","category":"reserves","matters":"Recurring spend funded from one-off reserves is unsustainable; when the Crisis Resilience Fund is exhausted, the council faces a structural deficit in children's in-need services.","figures":"Section 17 forecast overspend \u00a30.208m (with reserve); underlying overspend \u00a30.958m (without reserve); Crisis Resilience Fund drawdown \u00a30.750m","quote":"This position assumes \u00a30.750m from the Crisis and Resilience Fund. Without this, section 17 would be forecasting an overspend of \u00a30.958m.","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-08","committee":"Cabinet","paper_title":"Appendix 1 - Service Narrative","paper_url":"https://walthamforest.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110307/110307.pdf","headline":"School maintained balances fell \u00a33.987m to \u00a31.789m, with deficit balances now \u00a36.590m across 19 schools (43% in deficit)","detail":"Maintained schools' total balances dropped sharply year-on-year. Deficit balances increased by \u00a33.409m to \u00a36.590m, with 14 primary schools (48%), 3 secondary schools (43%), 1 special school, and 1 all-through school reporting deficits. Primary schools alone are in deficit by \u00a34.412m.","category":"demand","matters":"School deficits are an early signal of financial distress in the education sector; if these schools require council support or if DSG deficits are not resolved, they will transfer to the council's general fund.","figures":"Total school balances \u00a31.789m (down \u00a33.987m); deficit balances \u00a36.590m (up \u00a33.409m); 19 schools in deficit; primary deficits \u00a34.412m","quote":"A total of 19 Waltham Schools reported a deficit revenue balance at the end of the 2025/26 financial year. Deficit revenue balances represented a total value of \u00a36.590m, an increase of \u00a33.409m since 31st March 2025.","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-08"},"name":"Waltham Forest","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-26","since_iso":"2026-08-27","distress":true,"headline":"Agency spend reduced by \u00a37.678m in 2025/26, reaching six-year low of \u00a317.985m","lead":"A lender needs evidence that a council can execute cost-reduction programs and maintain financial discipline on major expense categories. This demonstrates Waltham Forest has achieved sustained, material savings through improved governance and strategic\u2026","also":["44 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Cabinet on 2026-10-06","n_findings":45,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Contract Award Agency Procurement Platform - Cabinet report","top_paper_url":"https://walthamforest.moderngov.co.uk/documents/s110301/110301.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":129,"news_drafts_total":3}}